<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="/css/site-wide.css">
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="/css/index.css">
    <link rel="icon" type="image/svg+xml" href="/assets/lug-light.svg">

    <title>Lug</title>
</head>
<body>
    {% include "header.njk" %}

    <main>
        <section id="hero" class="page-section">
            <img src="/assets/lug-light.svg" id="hero-sideicon" alt="Lug icon">

            <div class="hero-info">
                <h1>Lug</h1>
                <p>
                    A simple, embeddable, easy-to-learn language that leans heavily 
                    into the 'box' metaphor.
                </p>
                <a id="releases-button" class="button button-prominent" href="https://github.com/skribster/Lug/releases" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Releases</a>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section id="about" class="page-section">
            <div class="about-info">
                <h1>About</h1>
                <p>
                    <strong>Take Lua's simple syntax and mix it with Python's intuitiveness.</strong>
                    Lug is based on the 'box' metaphor commonly used to teach beginner 
                    programming concepts, with the language's semantics aiming to be 
                    predictable and consistent to newcomers.
                </p>
            </div>
            
            <aside>
                <pre class="code-snippet"><code><pre><code><span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">"Hello, world!"</span>)

<span class="kw">box</span> Dog <span class="op">&lt;-</span> <span class="kw">thing</span>
Dog.<span class="fn">new</span> <span class="op">&lt;-</span> <span class="kw">function</span>(name, age, sound)
    <span class="kw">box</span> newDog <span class="op">&lt;-</span> <span class="kw">thing</span> <span class="kw">of</span> Dog
    newDog.name <span class="op">&lt;-</span> name
    newDog.age <span class="op">&lt;-</span> age
    newDog.sound <span class="op">&lt;-</span> sound

    <span class="kw">return</span> newDog
<span class="kw">end</span>

Dog.<span class="fn">speak</span> <span class="op">&lt;-</span> <span class="kw">function</span>(<span class="kw">shared</span> self)
    <span class="fn">print</span>(self.name <span class="op">+</span> <span class="str">" says "</span> <span class="op">+</span> self.sound <span class="op">+</span> <span class="str">"!"</span>)
<span class="kw">end</span>

<span class="kw">box</span> buddy <span class="op">&lt;-</span> Dog.<span class="fn">new</span>(<span class="str">"Buddy"</span>, <span class="num">8</span>, <span class="str">"bark"</span>)
buddy:<span class="fn">speak</span>()</code></pre></code></pre>
            </aside>
        </section>

        <section id="why" class="page-section">
            <h1>Why Lug?</h1>
            <ul class="why-reasons card-grid">
                <li class="why-reason card">
                    <h2>Lug is easy to follow.</h2>
                    <p>
                        Lug is designed to feel approachable without making your code's 
                        behavior a mystery. Its central 'box' metaphor gives beginners
                        and newcomers a simple and concrete way to visualize how values 
                        are passed around. All with a friendly and readable syntax.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li class="why-reason card">
                    <h2>Lug is embeddable.</h2>
                    <p>
                        Lug is built in the widely-available C++17 standard, making it
                        embeddable in most modern C++ projects. Any application can create
                        a Lug virtual machine, utliizing its friendly C++ API, and run Lug 
                        code without relying on the standalone interpreter.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li class="why-reason card">
                    <h2>Lug is small.</h2>
                    <p>
                        Lug aims to keep its language and runtime focused rather than 
                        overwhelming users with a large, complicated system. Lug's binaries 
                        are less than half of a megabyte. The source code is also written in 
                        plain C++ without too much of the complicated or obscure syntax that 
                        may plague other C++ projects today. 
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li class="why-reason card">
                    <h2>Lug is powerful.</h2>
                    <p>
                        Lug is small, but that doesn't stop it from being powerful. Although
                        Lug is prototypal in its core design, it supports a multitude of 
                        paradigms while allowing more complex ways to interact with your
                        program. For instance, there is a simple but powerful structure in Lug called a 
                        'thing', but it lets you have complex metaprogramming and even a familiar
                        OOP structure.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        {% include "footer.njk" %}
    </main>
</body>
</html>